1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,725
The college canteen sells
three snacks and two drinks.

2
00:00:03,725 --> 00:00:07,835
How many different combos of one
snack and one drink can you order?

3
00:00:08,235 --> 00:00:09,730
Most people say five.

4
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:10,874
Three plus two.

5
00:00:10,874 --> 00:00:13,174
But count them, and you get six.

6
00:00:13,174 --> 00:00:16,027
Add one more drink, and it jumps to nine.

7
00:00:16,477 --> 00:00:18,884
Diya spotted this at the canteen today.

8
00:00:19,234 --> 00:00:22,102
Why do the choices multiply, and not add?

9
00:00:22,602 --> 00:00:26,392
The answer opens a new chapter,
Relations and Functions.

10
00:00:26,392 --> 00:00:29,135
Its first idea is the Cartesian product.

11
00:00:29,785 --> 00:00:32,476
Let us build every combo, one at a time.

12
00:00:32,826 --> 00:00:36,088
Set S holds the snacks:
samosa, vada and puff.

13
00:00:36,088 --> 00:00:38,795
Set D holds the drinks: tea and juice.

14
00:00:39,195 --> 00:00:40,404
Pick the samosa.

15
00:00:40,404 --> 00:00:42,891
It can go with tea, or with juice.

16
00:00:42,891 --> 00:00:44,454
That makes two combos.

17
00:00:44,854 --> 00:00:45,927
Pick the vada.

18
00:00:45,927 --> 00:00:48,414
Two drinks again, so two more combos.

19
00:00:48,814 --> 00:00:49,928
Pick the puff.

20
00:00:49,928 --> 00:00:50,828
Two more.

21
00:00:50,737 --> 00:00:52,857
Three snacks, two drinks each.

22
00:00:52,857 --> 00:00:54,228
Six combos in all.

23
00:00:54,878 --> 00:00:57,173
Now put the six combos on a grid.

24
00:00:57,173 --> 00:00:59,005
Snacks go along the bottom.

25
00:00:59,005 --> 00:01:00,596
Drinks go up the side.

26
00:01:00,996 --> 00:01:04,540
Each dot, where a column
meets a row, is one combo.

27
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:06,969
Three columns, two rows, six dots.

28
00:01:07,369 --> 00:01:09,469
Now the canteen adds a drink.

29
00:01:09,469 --> 00:01:10,723
Coffee comes in.

30
00:01:10,723 --> 00:01:13,189
One more row, so three more dots.

31
00:01:13,589 --> 00:01:15,603
Then lemonade, then buttermilk.

32
00:01:15,603 --> 00:01:18,322
Every new drink adds a whole row of three.

33
00:01:18,722 --> 00:01:22,803
So the number of combos is always
three, times the number of drinks.

34
00:01:23,453 --> 00:01:25,695
This set of all combos has a name.

35
00:01:26,045 --> 00:01:31,087
In NCERT words: the Cartesian product
of two non empty sets P and Q is

36
00:01:31,087 --> 00:01:35,155
the set of all ordered pairs
of elements from P and Q.

37
00:01:35,555 --> 00:01:37,231
We write it P cross Q.

38
00:01:37,231 --> 00:01:39,956
Each element is a pair in round brackets.

39
00:01:39,956 --> 00:01:43,377
First comes something from
P, then something from Q.

40
00:01:43,777 --> 00:01:48,976
In set builder form, P cross Q is
the set of all pairs p comma q, such

41
00:01:48,976 --> 00:01:52,190
that p belongs to P, and q belongs to Q.

42
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:55,266
Now, why do the choices multiply?

43
00:01:55,266 --> 00:01:57,759
Let us prove it in four small steps.

44
00:01:58,109 --> 00:01:59,009
Step one.

45
00:01:58,937 --> 00:02:02,594
Say set A has p elements,
and set B has q elements.

46
00:02:03,344 --> 00:02:04,244
Step two.

47
00:02:04,208 --> 00:02:05,895
Fix one element of A.

48
00:02:05,895 --> 00:02:09,005
It pairs with each of the q elements of B.

49
00:02:09,005 --> 00:02:10,523
That gives q pairs.

50
00:02:11,273 --> 00:02:12,202
Step three.

51
00:02:12,202 --> 00:02:15,647
Every element of A gets
its own column of q pairs.

52
00:02:15,647 --> 00:02:19,526
No pair repeats, because their
first entries are different.

53
00:02:20,276 --> 00:02:21,184
Step four.

54
00:02:21,184 --> 00:02:24,084
p columns, with q pairs in each column.

55
00:02:24,084 --> 00:02:27,432
That is q added p times,
which is p times q.

56
00:02:28,182 --> 00:02:31,484
So n of A cross B equals
n of A times n of B.

57
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:34,272
Three snacks, times two drinks, is six.

58
00:02:34,922 --> 00:02:36,032
Side by side.

59
00:02:36,032 --> 00:02:38,784
The union puts every item into one set.

60
00:02:38,784 --> 00:02:42,071
S and D share nothing,
so that is five items.

61
00:02:42,471 --> 00:02:46,431
The Cartesian product pairs
every snack with every drink.

62
00:02:46,431 --> 00:02:48,607
Three times two, six pairs.

63
00:02:48,607 --> 00:02:49,608
Union adds.

64
00:02:49,608 --> 00:02:51,327
The product multiplies.

65
00:02:51,927 --> 00:02:52,974
Now you try.

66
00:02:52,974 --> 00:02:56,277
The canteen runs out of
drinks, so D is empty.

67
00:02:56,277 --> 00:02:58,106
How many combos are left?

68
00:02:58,106 --> 00:02:59,356
Pause and think.

69
00:03:02,556 --> 00:03:03,456
Zero!

70
00:03:03,143 --> 00:03:05,902
A combo needs one snack and one drink.

71
00:03:05,902 --> 00:03:09,468
If either set is empty,
the product is empty too.

72
00:03:10,118 --> 00:03:11,190
Remember this.

73
00:03:11,190 --> 00:03:15,820
In a Cartesian product, every element
meets every element, so the choices

74
00:03:15,820 --> 00:03:16,720
multiply.

75
00:03:17,282 --> 00:03:19,017
Now, one worked example.

76
00:03:19,367 --> 00:03:22,378
A is the set of natural
numbers less than three.

77
00:03:22,378 --> 00:03:25,296
B is the set of integers
whose square is four.

78
00:03:25,296 --> 00:03:28,354
Find A cross B, and the
number of its elements.

79
00:03:28,854 --> 00:03:29,754
Step one.

80
00:03:29,657 --> 00:03:31,028
A is one and two.

81
00:03:31,028 --> 00:03:33,634
Remember, natural numbers start at one.

82
00:03:34,384 --> 00:03:35,284
Step two.

83
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:39,045
Two squared is four, and minus
two squared is also four.

84
00:03:39,045 --> 00:03:41,029
So B is minus two and two.

85
00:03:41,779 --> 00:03:42,717
Step three.

86
00:03:42,717 --> 00:03:46,033
Pair every element of A
with every element of B.

87
00:03:46,033 --> 00:03:47,339
Watch the arrows.

88
00:03:48,089 --> 00:03:48,989
Step four.

89
00:03:48,980 --> 00:03:54,061
A cross B is one comma minus two, one
comma two, two comma minus two, and

90
00:03:54,061 --> 00:03:55,219
two comma two.

91
00:03:55,969 --> 00:03:56,869
Step five.

92
00:03:56,840 --> 00:03:58,071
Check the count.

93
00:03:58,071 --> 00:04:01,425
Two times two is four
pairs, and we listed four.

94
00:04:02,075 --> 00:04:03,541
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:03,891 --> 00:04:05,209
A is one and two.

96
00:04:05,209 --> 00:04:06,667
B is three and four.

97
00:04:06,667 --> 00:04:10,931
Many students pair them like a
zip: one with three, two with four.

98
00:04:10,931 --> 00:04:12,042
Only two pairs.

99
00:04:12,442 --> 00:04:15,083
But every element must meet every element.

100
00:04:15,083 --> 00:04:17,830
One also meets four, and two meets three.

101
00:04:17,830 --> 00:04:19,155
Four pairs in all.

102
00:04:19,805 --> 00:04:21,374
Where is this asked?

103
00:04:21,374 --> 00:04:26,426
In PUC exams and in JEE Main, often
as: find the number of elements, or

104
00:04:26,426 --> 00:04:29,195
the number of subsets, of A cross B.

105
00:04:29,795 --> 00:04:31,019
Exam level now.

106
00:04:31,019 --> 00:04:33,541
A is the set of factors of twelve.

107
00:04:33,541 --> 00:04:37,453
B is the set of integers whose
square is less than five.

108
00:04:37,453 --> 00:04:40,088
A cross B has two to the k subsets.

109
00:04:40,088 --> 00:04:40,988
Find k.

110
00:04:40,836 --> 00:04:42,112
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:47,512 --> 00:04:48,711
The answer is C.

112
00:04:48,711 --> 00:04:49,611
Thirty.

113
00:04:49,825 --> 00:04:53,708
Twelve has six factors: one,
two, three, four, six and twelve.

114
00:04:54,208 --> 00:04:57,243
For B, do not forget
zero and the negatives.

115
00:04:57,243 --> 00:04:59,924
Minus two up to two gives five integers.

116
00:05:00,424 --> 00:05:04,155
So A cross B has six times
five, which is thirty elements.

117
00:05:04,655 --> 00:05:08,816
A set with thirty elements has
two to the power thirty subsets.

118
00:05:08,816 --> 00:05:10,068
So k is thirty.

119
00:05:10,668 --> 00:05:12,132
Here is your recap.

120
00:05:12,132 --> 00:05:13,637
Screenshot this card.

121
00:05:16,837 --> 00:05:19,674
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

122
00:05:20,074 --> 00:05:20,974
One.

123
00:05:20,565 --> 00:05:22,955
A has four elements and B has five.

124
00:05:22,955 --> 00:05:25,489
How many elements does A cross B have?

125
00:05:26,389 --> 00:05:27,289
Two.

126
00:05:26,853 --> 00:05:31,190
Write A cross B, when A is x and
y, and B is one, two and three.

127
00:05:32,090 --> 00:05:32,990
Three.

128
00:05:32,713 --> 00:05:35,665
Why is A cross the empty set always empty?

129
00:05:36,765 --> 00:05:41,655
Next time: exam hall seat three comma
five is not seat five comma three.

130
00:05:41,655 --> 00:05:44,167
Why does the order change everything?

131
00:05:44,167 --> 00:05:46,270
We find out in Ordered Pairs.
